            <p>Multiply the price by 0.80 (1 − 0.20). For example, 20% off £50 = £50 × 0.80 = £40. You save £10. Or use the calculator above — enter £50 and select 20%.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How do I calculate 30% off?</summary>
            <p>Multiply the price by 0.70. For example, 30% off £120 = £120 × 0.70 = £84. You save £36.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How do I find the original price from a sale price?</summary>
            <p>Divide the sale price by (1 − discount/100). If an item is £72 after a 10% discount, the original price was £72 ÷ 0.90 = £80.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What is the difference between percentage off and fixed discount?</summary>
            <p>A percentage discount (e.g. 20% off) scales with the price — the higher the original price, the more you save. A fixed discount (e.g. £10 off) gives the same saving regardless of the original price. Use the toggle above to switch between both types.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>If I apply two discounts, do they stack?</summary>
            <p>No, discounts applied sequentially do not simply add up. A 20% discount followed by a 10% discount gives 28% total off — not 30%. The second discount applies to the already-reduced price.</p>
